{
  "id": "group-anagrams",
  "title": "Group Anagrams",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "categories": ["hash-map", "string"],
  "descriptionMd": "Given an array of strings `strs`, gather the strings into groups such that two strings share a group exactly when one is a rearrangement of the other's letters (they are **anagrams**). Return the list of groups. The order of the groups, and the order of strings within each group, does not matter. If a word appears more than once, every copy goes into its group.\n\n**Example 1**\n\n```\nstrs = [\"tea\", \"ant\", \"eat\", \"tan\", \"bat\", \"ate\"]\n→ [[\"tea\", \"eat\", \"ate\"], [\"ant\", \"tan\"], [\"bat\"]]\n```\n\n**Example 2**\n\n```\nstrs = [\"\"]\n→ [[\"\"]]\n```\n\n**Example 3**\n\n```\nstrs = [\"z\"]\n→ [[\"z\"]]\n```",
  "constraints": ["1 ≤ strs.length ≤ 10⁴", "0 ≤ strs[i].length ≤ 100", "strs[i] contains only lowercase English letters"],
  "hints": ["Anagrams look identical once you ignore letter order. What canonical form could every member of a group share?", "Use that canonical form as a key into a hash map of groups.", "With only 26 possible letters, a letter-count signature can replace sorting."],
  "signature": {
    "functionName": "groupAnagrams",
    "params": [{ "name": "strs", "type": "string[]" }],
    "returns": "string[][]"
  },
  "comparator": "unorderedNested",
  "tests": [
    { "input": [["tea", "ant", "eat", "tan", "bat", "ate"]], "expected": [["tea", "eat", "ate"], ["ant", "tan"], ["bat"]] },
    { "input": [[""]], "expected": [[""]] },
    { "input": [["z"]], "expected": [["z"]] },
    { "input": [["", ""]], "expected": [["", ""]], "hidden": true },
    { "input": [["ab", "ba", "ab"]], "expected": [["ab", "ba", "ab"]], "hidden": true },
